            <div xml:id="d396">

                <pb facs="0308" n="304" sameAs="frus:frus1938v04/pg304"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1115/3055: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-04-04T13:00:00+09:00">April 4, 1938—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 4—5:43 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>225. Department’s 116, April 1, 9 p.m., return of Americans to Nanking. I called
                    today on the Vice Minister for Foreign Affairs and <pb facs="0309" n="305"
                        xml:id="pg305"/> made strong representations supported by an <hi
                        rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi> as well as a separate informal
                    document presenting the facts described in Nanking’s 90, April 1, 4 p.m. The
                    situation was fully and vigorously set forth.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Shanghai for Nanking.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
